\begin{abstract}
Deep learning technology has been widely applied in the processing of multi-source blended data, including the deblending of blended data and the interpolation of missing traces. However, the need for a large amount of labeled data as training sets has somewhat limited the use of these methods. Typically, unsupervised training, which does not rely on labeled data, often employs generative networks, leading to significantly long training times. To address this, we have developed a self-supervised training method that simultaneously performs interpolation and deblending. This approach constructs a composite loss function based on data characteristics, effectively suppressing missing data and blending noise. Through numerical tests on both synthetic and real data, the results demonstrate that the proposed method can achieve self-supervised training without relying on extensive datasets, successfully accomplishing simultaneous interpolation and separation of blended data.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The key of simultaneous source seismic exploration technology lies in artificially shortening the source excitation interval, causing seismic records to overlap and thereby increasing acquisition efficiency. When combined with compressed sensing theory for irregularly deployed receivers at the recording end, the acquisition cost of seismic exploration can be further reduced. However, this approach introduces two main challenges: first, the blending of seismic records, and second, the regularization issues caused by missing traces. Currently, the deblending of blended data primarily relies on iterative methods, where partial effective signals are predicted from the blended data and then subtracted from the blended records to gradually obtain clean seismic records. Methods for predicting effective signals generally include those based on compressed sensing theory constraints and those based on deep learning. Similar to the separation task, seismic data interpolation also follows the above process. Therefore, we have integrated the separation and interpolation tasks to develop a self-supervised training method for simultaneous interpolation and separation. This method offers faster training speeds compared to generative self-supervised training and better generalization than supervised training, enabling it to simultaneously accomplish interpolation and separation tasks. We tested the algorithm using blended data generated from the Marmousi velocity model and real data to validate the method's effectiveness.

\section*{Theory}

\subsubsection{Deblending and Interpolation}

Treat blended data as noisy data, the blended data can discribed as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{D}:\left[
\begin{array}{c}
    \mathbf{d}_{1_{ble}}\\
    \vdots           \\
    \mathbf{d}_{n_{ble}}
\end{array}
\right]_{(n\times1)}=
\mathbf{m}:\left[
\begin{array}{c}
    \mathbf{d}_1 \\
    \vdots       \\
    \mathbf{d}_n
\end{array}
\right]_{(n\times1)}+
\mathbf{Ble}:\left[
\begin{array}{c}
    \mathbf{ble}_1 \\
    \vdots       \\
    \mathbf{ble}_n
\end{array}
\right]_{(n\times1)}
\label{eq:D=m+ble}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{d}_{k_{ble}}$is the blended data of the $n$th source, $\mathbf{D}$ represents all sources' blended data, $\mathbf{d}_n$ means the $n$th clean seismic data.$\mathbf{m}$ represents all sources' clean data. $\mathbf{ble}_n$ denotes the blend noise in the $n$th blended data, also, $\mathbf{Ble}$ represents all blend noise.
%\plot{ts}{width=1\columnwidth}{Tower-streamer bilateral simultaneous excitation }

Unlike noisy seismic data, blended data contains multiple source signals in a single record. The individual source signals in the data are coherent with each other. The blended data can be described by the following equation:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dp=td}
\mathbf{ble}_{n}=\sum_{i=1,i\neq n}^{N}\Gamma_{n,i}\mathbf{d}_i
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma_{n,i}$ repersents the $i$th source signal blended with $n$th source signal operate. The $\Gamma_{n,i}$ can be discribed as equation:
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_{n,i}\mathbf{d}(x,t)=\mathbf{d}(x,t+\Delta{t}_{n,i})
\end{equation}
where $\Delta{t}_{n,i}$repersent the $i$th source shot delay time for $n$th source, $\mathbf{d}$ is a seismic gathers. On this basis, the missing trace can be represented by a sampling matrix. Rewriting equation~\ref{eq:dp=td} like equation~\ref{eq:D=m+ble} and include the missing traces data:

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
\mathbf{D}=\mathbf{F}\cdot (R \times \mathbf{m});\\
\mathbf{F}:\left[
\begin{array}{cccc}
    \mathbf{I}         &\Gamma_{2,1} &\cdots      &\Gamma_{n,1}     \\
    \Gamma_{1,2}       &\ddots        &\ddots     &\vdots           \\
    \vdots             &\ddots       &\ddots      &\vdots           \\
    \Gamma_{1,n}       &\cdots       &\cdots      &\mathbf{I}
\end{array}\right]_{(n\times{n})} 
\label{eq:D=Tm_mx}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

Here, $\mathbf{R}$ represents a 0-1 matrix that marks the missing traces. $\mathbf{F}$ represents the blending operator matrix. Based on this formula, deriving $\mathbf{m}$ from $\mathbf{D}$ forms the core idea of the inversion-based separation iteration. As long as we can recover a portion of the valid signal from the pseudo-deblended\cite[]{mahdadSeparationBlendedData2011} records, we can use the blending formula to reverse-delay the valid signal back into the blended noise, subtract it from the pseudo-deblended records, and obtain cleaner pseudo-deblended records. This iterative process continues until the noise is completely suppressed.

\subsubsection{Iterative Framework}

The blended data in a gather, which is not a common source gather, contains source signals arranged in a continuous shape for the main source, while the other sources can be considered as random noise. However, the noise in the blended data is delayed by $\Gamma$ from the useful record, meaning the position of the noise in the pseudo-deblended record can be predicted. Based on this characteristic, \cite{mahdad2011} introduced a method called the estimation and subtraction algorithm. Regardless of the operation used to obtain a clean record, the recorded data can be reversed to blend the noise, which is then subtracted from the pseudo-separated record to initiate the next iterative process. This approach maximizes the protection of the active signal from degradation. The traditional iterative method can be described as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{m_{i+1}} = \mathbf{S}(\mathbf{D}-(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{F})\mathbf{R} \times \mathbf{m_i}+(1-\mathbf{R}) \times \mathbf{m_i})
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathbf{I}$ represents the identity matrix, $\mathbf{S}$ srepresents a coherence constraint operation, which is used to separate part of the clean signal $\mathbf{m}$ from the blended data $\mathbf{D}$, the $i$ means $i$th iterative times. In the existing iterative deblending operation, $\mathbf{m}$ is one seismic traces gather. This method is then applied to all seismic trace gathers to obtain a single seismic signal. However, this approach faces challenges when the differences between the primary and secondary sources are too small in certain regions of the data domain, as the coherence constraint $S$ struggles to effectively separate the blended data. 

\subsubsection{Self-supervised training}
%上述迭代框架中的关键是约束算子S的构建，S需要同时完成混叠噪声压制与缺失地震道重建的功能。采用深度学习的方法来设计约束算子，相较于使用压缩感知理论的稀疏约束、低秩约束等物理驱动方法更加的快捷，对于数据的结构，我们设计了如下的混叠损失函数。
The key to the aforementioned iterative framework lies in the construction of the constraint operator $S$, which must simultaneously achieve the functions of suppressing blended noise and reconstructing missing seismic traces. Designing the constraint operator using deep learning methods is significantly faster compared to physics-driven approaches such as sparse constraints or low-rank constraints based on compressive sensing theory. To address the structure of the data, we have designed the following blending loss function.
%缺失恢复损的约束，我们通过重采样人为设置新的确实地震道R_m，R_m和原始缺失地震道R不重合，将重采样后的数据输入网络得到输出，输出和原始输入的初始非缺失部位进行损失函数计算，即使用原始数据中的非缺失部位作为训练集训练，损失函数可写为如下格式：
For the constraint of missing data recovery, we artificially create new missing seismic traces $\mathbf{R_m}$ through resampling, ensuring that 
$\mathbf{R_m}$ does not overlap with the original missing traces $\mathbf{R}$. The resampled data is then fed into the network, and the output is compared with the original input's initially non-missing portions to compute the loss function. In other words, the non-missing portions of the original data are used as the training set. The loss function can be expressed in the following form:
\begin{equation}
\text{}{L}_{i}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta})= \Vert ((1-\mathbf{R}) \times \mathscr{F}(\mathbf{R_m} \times \mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta}) - \mathbf{X})\Vert^{2}
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathbf{X}$ represents the input data to the network, which is the data to be processed containing both blending noise and missing traces. $\mathscr{F}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta})$means using parameters $\mathbf{\theta}$ to predict clean data from $\mathbf{X}$, For the deblending task, we design the constraint based on the forward blending formula. The output of the network is treated as the clean data, which is then re-blended and compared with the input data to compute the loss function. This approach enables self-supervised training. The loss function can be expressed in the following format:
\begin{equation}
\text{}{L}_{d}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta})= \Vert \mathbf{F} \cdot (\mathscr{F}\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta}) - \mathbf{X}\Vert^{2}
\end{equation}
Combining the two constraints mentioned above, the final loss function is as follows:
\begin{equation}
\text{}{L}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta})= \Vert (1-\mathbf{R}) \times (\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathscr{F}(\mathbf{R_m} \times \mathbf{X},\mathbf{\theta}) - \mathbf{X})\Vert^{2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Iterative training}
To maximize the network's suppression of strong interference, we integrate the iterative framework into the training process. When the number of training epochs exceeds half, based on the change in the loss value, we save the network's output data whenever the loss value decreases. We then process the input using the formula to obtain a new input $\mathbf{x_{i+1}}$. Compared to the result of the previous iteration $\mathbf{x_{i}}$, the missing traces in $\mathbf{x_{i+1}}$ will be partially recovered, while the blended noise will be suppressed. This reduces the interference in the input data, making it easier for the network to learn the effective components in the data, thus improving the accuracy of the output. This process can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x_{i+1}} = \mathscr{F}(\mathbf{D}-(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{F})\mathbf{R} \times \mathbf{x_i}+(1-\mathbf{R}) \times \mathbf{x_i},\theta)
\end{equation}
Since the network training iteration does not always decrease along the optimal direction, during the training process, the following judgment is made for each iteration:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{m_{i+1}} = \arg\min_{\mathbf{m} \in {\mathbf{m_{i+1}}, \mathbf{m_{i}}}} \text{}{L}(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{\theta})
\end{equation}

\subsection{Examples}
We utilized a dataset generated from forward modeling of the Marmousi velocity model and a set of field data to conduct separate self-supervised interpolation, self-supervised deblending, and simultaneous interpolation and separation training. The results are illustrated in the figure .

As shown in the figure, the proposed method achieves satisfactory results for both standalone interpolation and deblending tasks, while also demonstrating excellent performance in the simultaneous interpolation and separation task. The network training was conducted using four Quadro GP100 GPUs, with WUDTnet serving as the reference network\cite[]{ke2024hybrid}. The total training time was approximately 200 minutes. Compared to generative networks such as diffusion models\cite[]{wangSeisFusionConstrainedDiffusion2024}, this approach requires significantly less time and can be adapted to various simple network architectures.



\subsection{Conclusions}
The simultaneous interpolation and deblending method for blended data proposed in this paper addresses the self-supervised deblending and interpolation problem using neural networks by leveraging data structure and designing a composite loss function. By introducing a novel loss function, the method enables self-supervised training while effectively performing accurate interpolation during the processing of blended data, ensuring high-fidelity reconstruction and providing a feasible solution for improving data quality in various practical application scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared to mainstream generative networks, the proposed method enhances computational efficiency and broadens applicability, offering significant theoretical and practical value. In the context of blended data separation, the proposed loss function does not achieve perfect results when dealing with lower-quality data. To address this, targeted network innovations, such as incorporating blind traces or masks to assist in self-supervised training, could be explored.
